WEBVTT

00:00:00.141 --> 00:00:03.967
Transactions let us group changes into a controlled unit of work.

00:00:03.967 --> 00:00:06.332
BEGIN starts the transaction.

00:00:06.332 --> 00:00:12.212
After that, changes are visible inside the transaction but have not yet been permanently kept.

00:00:12.212 --> 00:00:14.177
COMMIT keeps the changes. ROLLBACK discards them.

00:00:14.177 --> 00:00:18.932
In this example, we change a value, inspect the changed row, and then roll the transaction back.

00:00:18.932 --> 00:00:22.957
Before running it, note the original age.

00:00:22.957 --> 00:00:24.337
Then run the code and follow the output.

00:00:24.337 --> 00:00:31.017
You should see the temporary changed value, and after the rollback the final SELECT should show the original

00:00:31.017 --> 00:00:32.452
value again. This is a safe way to practice write statements.

00:00:32.452 --> 00:00:39.842
In pgAdmin, transactions are also useful when testing a change before deciding whether the result should be

00:00:39.842 --> 00:00:44.922
committed. Transactions let several statements behave as one logical unit.

00:00:44.922 --> 00:00:49.307
That matters when partial success would leave the database inconsistent—for example,

00:00:49.307 --> 00:00:54.217
creating an order header without its order lines or moving money out of one account without recording the

00:00:54.217 --> 00:00:58.000
corresponding destination. COMMIT confirms the unit; ROLLBACK abandons it.
